  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft has transitioned to primarily zonal flow \ndownstream of the deep low pressure system that continues to reside \nover the Pacific Northwest. Near to the surface, the pattern is \nnearly the same when accounting for tilting with height. The \nejection of the low seems to be a tad slower than previously \nforecasted and may delay the impact on Saturday's weather somewhat. \nIn the meantime, Friday will look similar with highs in the 60s and \na breezy south wind at around 25 mph and gusts up to 35 mph. \n\nAs the system approaches the CWA, the dry and warm pattern will \nfinally come to an end. With the lagging of the progged cold front \nsomewhat, Saturday highs are expected to reach close to the 60s \nbefore significant CAA cools the area significantly. Additionally \nwith the cold front, precipitation is forecast with its passage. \nChances remain similar with >20% chance east of Highway 283 and the \nbest chances (40-60%) closer to central Kansas. The latest from \nforecast soundings show that there is enough CAPE and and moisture \nthat marginally severe storms can not be ruled out and the SPC has \nan early marginal risk for the southeastern portion of the CWA.\n\nHigh temperatures Sunday through the rest of the forecast period are \nforecast around 40-45 degrees. The other notable aspect of the \nforecast period worth monitoring is the precipitation and snow \nchances Tuesday morning. Although temperatures during the snow fall \nmay hover around 28-30 degrees, between the light snow and the \nrelatively warm ground accumulations are not expected. That said, \nslick spots are possible so travel precautions are strongly advised. \nThe forecast will have a better grasp of amounts as the event draws \nnear, but the early indications are that amounts will primarily stay \nbelow an inch. Ensembles suggest that another round of light \nprecipitation is possible at the very end of the forecast period \nnext Friday, but any details would be futile with the amount of \nremaining uncertainty. &&",
